     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose isn't safe.** Amlodipine 50 mg once daily is five times the usual maximum of 10 mg per day. Adults normally take 5 mg once daily, or 2.5 mg in some patients, and the dose can go up to 10 mg. At 50 mg, the patient could develop dangerously low blood pressure, shock, and swelling. Calcium channel blocker overdoses can be hard to reverse. Most likely someone meant 5 mg and the number got mistyped or misheard somewhere.\n\n**2. The order needs to come from a prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders must be entered or authorized by a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care. This is both a hospital policy and a legal requirement, so I can't enter it on your behalf no matter what the dose is.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team on call so they can confirm what was intended.\n- If a 50 mg order has already been written or passed along anywhere, flag it to pharmacy or the nurse caring for the patient before anyone gives a dose.\n\nIf the prescriber confirms the intended dose (for example, 5 mg by mouth once daily), I can enter it once they place or authorize the order."
      }
     ],
